1
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:20,810
Okay, everyone, we can begin.

2
00:00:20,810 --> 00:00:31,440
Yesterday, we covered Day 1, Sections 1 and 2.

3
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:59,520
After two sessions, you can complete a basic GTest task and discuss questions later.

4
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:16,310
Today, we will cover four practical topics in GTest.

5
00:01:16,310 --> 00:01:31,250
First, we will revisit test functions, static variables, and inline functions.

6
00:01:31,250 --> 00:01:49,410
Then, we will discuss complex MCDC and delivery for GTest-tested modules.

7
00:01:49,410 --> 00:02:13,620
For static variables, follow the syntax shown yesterday.

8
00:02:26,860 --> 00:02:42,040
If there are no questions, I will assume this part is clear.

9
00:02:42,040 --> 00:03:03,600
Next, let us discuss how to test inline functions.

10
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:16,360
Why do we need to test inline functions?

11
00:03:16,360 --> 00:03:28,090
An inline function is used to make execution faster.

12
00:03:28,090 --> 00:03:48,340
A test script may not recognize an inline function defined only in source code.

13
00:03:48,340 --> 00:04:05,840
As a result, testing can produce an undefined-function error.

14
00:04:05,840 --> 00:05:05,760
Declare the function in the header file, then test it normally from the test script.

15
00:05:05,760 --> 00:06:04,020
Do you have questions or have you encountered a similar inline-function issue?

16
00:06:04,020 --> 00:06:37,850
Another common issue occurs when an MCDC expression is too complex.

17
00:06:37,850 --> 00:06:59,450
A workaround is to exclude MCDC and test C0 and C1 for that function.

18
00:06:59,450 --> 00:07:54,230
Use the syntax shown here and replace the placeholder with the complex function name.

19
00:07:54,230 --> 00:08:05,800
Open the indicated configuration file and copy the script into it.

20
00:08:05,800 --> 00:08:17,800
Replace the placeholder with the function that contains the complex MCDC expression.

21
00:08:17,800 --> 00:08:37,290
After saving, clean and rebuild the project so the tool recognizes the change.

22
00:08:37,290 --> 00:08:46,090
You must rebuild from the beginning because the configuration is rebuilt internally.

23
00:08:46,090 --> 00:09:50,800
Refer to the guideline for these issues and their solutions, or contact me with questions.

24
00:09:50,800 --> 00:10:15,780
When you build the project, check that every console message is okay.

25
00:10:15,780 --> 00:10:44,160
Do not ignore a mock warning, because it can affect your code logic.

26
00:10:44,160 --> 00:11:07,700
Set every input strictly instead of leaving an uncontrolled return value.

27
00:11:07,700 --> 00:11:27,800
A report may show 100% coverage even though some values have not been checked.

28
00:11:27,800 --> 00:11:51,660
Please share any task issues so we can document their solutions.

29
00:11:51,660 --> 00:12:34,610
Since there are no questions, let us move on to delivery.

30
00:12:34,610 --> 00:12:59,160
For delivery, follow the structure shown on the slide.

31
00:12:59,160 --> 00:13:20,040
First, identify the C file and the folder level where it is located.

32
00:13:20,040 --> 00:13:35,640
Create a Unit Test folder with a TPA folder and a test folder inside it.

33
00:13:35,640 --> 00:13:47,010
The test folder should contain the zipped GTest environment for the module under test.

34
00:13:47,010 --> 00:14:06,110
Zip the environment before delivery to prevent Jenkins errors from project files or configuration.

35
00:14:06,110 --> 00:14:25,260
A reviewer can open the ZIP file and identify the test environment for that C file.

36
00:14:25,260 --> 00:14:45,680
Follow the naming and folder-level convention defined by your project for consistency.

37
00:14:45,680 --> 00:15:00,810
Do you have any questions about this delivery structure?

38
00:15:00,810 --> 00:15:24,340
How does GTest distinguish task IDs when several variants test the same C file?

39
00:15:24,340 --> 00:15:42,950
You mean the same C file may have several variants, correct?

40
00:15:42,950 --> 00:16:26,730
This depends on the organization; keep the appropriate BB or variant in a separate folder.

41
00:16:26,730 --> 00:16:46,570
Reviewers then open the required environment, enable the relevant variant, and run the test.

42
00:16:46,570 --> 00:17:00,230
For example, a C file with three variants can use three configurations in one test environment.

43
00:17:00,230 --> 00:17:34,980
Okay, thank you. Let us continue.

44
00:17:34,980 --> 00:18:17,600
We also need a way to distinguish special cases that use the same module name.

45
00:18:17,600 --> 00:18:50,940
Our internal process uses separate folders to distinguish configurations and reduce checking time.

46
00:18:50,940 --> 00:19:38,810
This differs from our process, where we rebuild tests after each development baseline change.

47
00:19:38,810 --> 00:20:25,720
The goal of retesting is to build as many useful test cases as possible and cover more scenarios.

48
00:20:25,720 --> 00:20:44,270
We reuse the previous tested cases and add new test cases to test the updated code.

49
00:20:44,270 --> 00:20:57,100
Why do we recreate the tests after a code update instead of reviewing the old tests?

50
00:20:57,100 --> 00:21:12,290
Our team often restarts because we do not receive the previous test cases or know how to review them.

51
00:21:12,290 --> 00:21:26,050
That can require far more effort than the small code change itself.

52
00:21:26,050 --> 00:22:47,530
We should standardize this delivery format so that previous results are easy to review.

53
00:22:47,530 --> 00:23:07,530
However, repeatedly running the same test cases may not detect newly introduced defects.

54
00:23:07,530 --> 00:23:16,760
If the code does not change, review it; if it changes, test only the changes.

55
00:23:16,760 --> 00:24:09,590
Teams often do not receive or store older test cases, so later testers cannot reuse them.

56
00:24:09,590 --> 00:24:52,060
Most earlier requests used Cantata, while current GTest requests are new and have no old test set.

57
00:24:52,060 --> 00:25:41,440
Cantata retesting is also difficult when the previous report cannot be viewed.

58
00:25:41,440 --> 00:26:09,930
Following this format makes the delivery process complete and lets others find the old test script.

59
00:26:09,930 --> 00:26:35,250
The TPA file supports validation and records verdicts, coverage, C0, C1, MCDC, comments, and reasons.

60
00:26:35,250 --> 00:27:12,200
I will send our TPA format after this session; keep comments and coverage data accurate.

61
00:27:12,200 --> 00:27:42,640
Incorrect TPA data can cause errors when it is stored on the server.

62
00:27:42,640 --> 00:27:58,650
Next, I will show how to validate and deliver a tested C module.

63
00:27:58,650 --> 00:28:35,680
In SharCC, search for the module and select the component that contains its proxy.

64
00:28:36,980 --> 00:28:51,380
Right-click the component and choose Open Validity Editor, then wait for the process to finish.

65
00:28:51,920 --> 00:28:54,780
Do not click Search before the process finishes, or the tool will report an error.

66
00:28:57,180 --> 00:29:28,400
When the editor is ready, enter the module name, choose the correct TPA folder, and validate it.

67
00:29:28,400 --> 00:29:39,260
A green check mark confirms successful validation and the completion of testing.

68
00:29:39,260 --> 00:30:00,080
That completes the delivery and validation process for the module.

69
00:30:00,080 --> 00:30:12,420
Do you have any questions about this part of the session?

70
00:30:12,420 --> 00:30:27,030
If a C file has several variants, how can we map it to several TPA files?

71
00:30:27,030 --> 00:30:45,060
The system displays only the C file that you search for validation.

72
00:30:45,060 --> 00:31:14,010
It treats validation as a one-to-one relationship between one C file and one TPA file.

73
00:31:14,010 --> 00:31:20,840
So we choose one variant rather than mapping all variants at once.

74
00:31:20,840 --> 00:31:50,180
Have you encountered any questions while practicing the example?

75
00:31:50,180 --> 00:32:18,490
The test process is straightforward; practical work only becomes difficult when specific issues occur.

76
00:32:18,490 --> 00:33:30,990
GTest is simply a framework that supports testing, with the same basic principles as before.

77
00:33:30,990 --> 00:33:39,090
Would you like me to demonstrate the delivery process in SharCC again?

78
00:33:39,090 --> 00:34:00,490
I can demonstrate the SharCC delivery process again, because this session is for everyone.

79
00:34:00,490 --> 00:34:17,130
Let me show the steps for working with SharCC.

80
00:34:17,130 --> 00:34:40,880
First, create a local folder for the repository.

81
00:34:40,880 --> 00:36:07,400
Open the folder in SharCC and connect to the project; you need access permission.

82
00:36:07,400 --> 00:36:43,800
For example, after connecting, the left panel shows all streams in the selected project.

83
00:36:43,800 --> 00:37:50,190
To deliver, select the target stream and create a local repository from it.

84
00:37:50,190 --> 00:38:29,480
Name the repository clearly for the task; it works like creating a child branch for delivery.

85
00:38:29,480 --> 00:38:53,820
Select the component that you want to deliver, such as IP AppNet.

86
00:38:53,820 --> 00:39:18,360
Always include the .project file because it contains the .logru configuration file.

87
00:39:18,360 --> 00:40:28,210
Select the .logru file as well, because it adapts server settings for your local machine.

88
00:40:28,210 --> 00:42:20,820
The local repository is created in the folder you chose and contains the files downloaded from the server.

89
00:42:20,820 --> 00:43:34,270
Before delivery, always get the latest version to avoid overwriting someone else's new files.

90
00:43:34,690 --> 00:44:16,620
Before you use Check In and Deliver, verify that every folder matches your latest version.

91
00:44:16,620 --> 00:44:27,300
That is the SharCC process for delivering a task.

92
00:44:34,370 --> 00:45:17,540
Have you encountered any issues with these steps in SharCC?

93
00:45:17,540 --> 00:45:31,460
Please raise any questions from yesterday's session, and I will answer them if I can.

94
00:45:31,460 --> 00:45:42,620
We have now completed the GTest Script Basic, Advanced, and Deliverable training.

95
00:45:42,620 --> 00:46:22,080
Please ask any questions about GTest Script while we are still in this final training session.

96
00:46:22,080 --> 00:47:04,380
I do not yet understand how to distinguish C0, C1, and MCDC when writing test cases.

97
00:47:06,100 --> 00:47:26,540
The documentation calls them statement and decision coverage, but I am still confused.

98
00:47:26,540 --> 00:47:47,570
Thank you for the question. I will explain C0, C1, and MCDC before test-case design.

99
00:47:47,930 --> 00:48:34,140
C0 is line coverage: it shows whether a line of code has been executed.

100
00:48:34,140 --> 00:49:08,850
C1 is branch coverage: it measures whether the logic branches have been executed.

101
00:49:08,850 --> 00:49:28,970
Achieving 100% C1 also covers C0, while 100% MCDC covers both C1 and C0.

102
00:49:28,970 --> 00:49:47,890
Does this explain the difference, or do you think 100% C0 and C1 guarantee 100% MCDC?

103
00:49:47,890 --> 00:51:51,510
No. Think of C0 inside C1, and both inside MCDC; covering MCDC can cover the others.

104
00:51:51,510 --> 00:51:58,890
You can read more on a wiki page, but this code example makes the difference easier to see.

105
00:51:58,890 --> 00:52:14,060
After understanding coverage, we can discuss how to create a meaningful test case.

106
00:52:14,060 --> 00:52:44,300
A test case has three main parts: preconditions, inputs, and the function call or output.

107
00:52:44,300 --> 00:53:15,620
The third part is checking the result after the function executes.

108
00:53:15,620 --> 00:53:54,380
Every test case must have a clear purpose, such as checking a condition at line 54.

109
00:53:54,380 --> 00:55:21,210
Create tests to expose defects and verify whether the code behaves as intended.

110
00:55:21,210 --> 00:55:46,420
When there are many tests, you should outline in advance which test cases you need.

111
00:55:46,420 --> 00:56:10,480
Choose appropriate techniques based on project requirements, experience, and the type of logic.

112
00:56:10,480 --> 00:56:37,800
Use your experience to create effective tests that can reveal a problem in the code.

113
00:56:37,800 --> 00:57:01,490
A test is effective when it deliberately exposes behavior that differs from the developer's expectation.

114
00:57:01,490 --> 00:58:46,580
Please post any remaining questions; we will wait about five minutes and end the training early if there are none.

115
00:58:46,580 --> 00:59:11,590
During a GTest Unit Testing task, write your questions in the chat so we can discuss them together.

116
00:59:11,590 --> 00:59:27,790
Could you share these materials in the external folder so we can use them for testing?

117
